From 2732d9d85f840a8865be83885eec369ae4616c98 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 13:18:58 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Better water under the new look: reflections, clear shallows,
 glint and foam

Lakes, rivers and the sea now look alive, as WoW Forever's do. The shore's
trees, hills and buildings are mirrored in the water, broken up by small
ripples, with the hour's sky where nothing stands; it is a mirror at
grazing angles and see-through looking down. The bed shows through in the
shallows, clear turquoise going to deep blue-green with depth; a soft
white foam line laps at the waterline and rings a hero's legs as they
wade; the sun glints sharp and warm on the ripples, which the bloom picks
up, and the moon lays a pale path at night. A sun shadow on the water dims
its glint and a little of its mirror. The water is glassy at dawn, dusk
and night and takes a breeze by day.

Between the opaque and the transparent passes, the world as it stands
and its depth are copied (one full-screen draw), and liquid.wgsl's new
look_water arm reads them: the length of water a pixel's ray crosses to
the bed sets how much of the bed shows, a mirrored ray is marched through
the copied depth to the shore (screen-space reflection, the sky colours
where it leaves the screen), and the frame's own alpha blend lays the
water over the bed, so anything drawn behind it still shows. The tuning
rides in a small water block each liquid material binds.

worldWater 0 keeps 1.12's water under the rest of the new look;
lookwater <reflect 0-2> [clarity 0-2] | grade holds the mirror and the
clarity for film; worldLook 0, and a camera under the water, run 1.12's
water untouched.

